<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">De Ceremoniis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 956–959</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as De Cerimoniis, De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae, Book of Ceremonies</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De Ceremoniis, in full De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae and in English the Book of Ceremonies, is a Greek book of the ceremonial protocol of the Byzantine emperors' court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, written in the middle of the 10th century. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> wrote or at least commissioned it, probably around 956–959, and it was partly revised under Nikephoros II; it includes chapters from a 6th-century manual by Peter the Patrician. It sets out the processions, stations, costumes and acclamations of religious feasts and of secular rites such as coronations, weddings, births and funerals, as well as the reception of ambassadors. An English translation in two volumes was published in 2012.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-ceremoniis.jpg" alt="De Ceremoniis" width="239" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For nearly a thousand years Hagia Sophia was the cathedral of Constantinople, the seat of its patriarch and a principal setting for imperial ceremonies such as coronations, which the emperor Constantine VII described in his <b>Book of Ceremonies</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor (basileus) was held to be chosen by God and to be the living law (nomos empsychos), and his court, whose ceremonies Constantine VII had set down in the <b>Book of Ceremonies</b>, was meant to mirror the order of heaven.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
